tonight we take a look at an incredible  effort made by members of our community  to help those in hospice just in time  for christmas  sasha loftus joining us now live near  jones and sunset with the story you'll  only see on 8 news now  [Music]  denise and brian these are the stories  we all need to hear right now  a mother and daughter duo heard that the  patients at this hospice care here  needed some help just in time for  christmas so they got to work  and their efforts brought more joy than  anyone could have imagined  many say christmas is a time for  miracles  [Music]  [Applause]  after weeks of work around the clock a  local family  made this one happen it took a village  and  you know the vegas community really came  through amber garcia and her daughter  ayanna castro  organized this amazing donation event  they gathered things like blankets  pajamas fuzzy socks  and handwritten cards dropping them here  at infinity hospice care  on friday we just reached out to all of  our  networks of friends and this is what  happened  170 patients who've had to spend months  alone due to covid19  can now all expect gifts under the tree  for the first time  in six years it's the first time  since i've been with this company in six  years that we've had  every single patient walk away with  something  20 20 has been tough this is so great  but nikki ellis of infinity hospice care  says  this gesture has the power to turn it  all around  whatever they went through back in the  past they could put that behind them  and they can see all the wonderfulness  that's going to come ahead  the community may not be able to show  its appreciation  up close but this tribute means so much  more  we want them to feel the happiness and  warmth and no matter how much  sadness they felt every single person  here  will leave this earth knowing they are  loved  that we care about them that they are  not alone  thanks to amber ayana and of course the  entire las vegas community every patient  here at infinity hospice will receive  things like pajamas socks  anything you can really imagine those  christmas cards just in time for the  holiday  the volunteers here will wrap them and  get them out a week from today  for now reporting live sasha loftis 8  news now
 

